<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kullervo Manner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kullervo Manner was a Finnish Social Democrat, speaker of parliament in 1917, who chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-peoples-delegation">Finnish People's Delegation</a>, the Red government during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> of 1918. After the Reds were defeated he fled to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and helped found the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, which he later led. He was arrested in Stalin's purges and died in a labour camp.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kullervo-manner.jpg" alt="Kullervo Manner" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kullervo_Manner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kullervo Achilles Manner was born on 12 October 1880 in Kokemäki, the son of the minister Gustaf Manner and of Alma Limón, a pastor's daughter. After leaving high school in 1900 he worked as a journalist in Porvoo and Helsinki. In 1906 he founded the Porvoo newspaper Työläinen (Worker) and edited it until 1909. An article it published in 1909 brought him, by then a member of parliament, a six-month prison sentence for lèse-majesté against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. His brother Arvo Manner later governed the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> and Kymi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Manner was elected to parliament for Uusimaa as a Social Democrat in 1910 and 1917, and chaired the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in 1917–1918. He was named chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-peoples-delegation">Finnish People's Delegation</a> on 28 January 1918. On 10 April 1918 he became commander-in-chief of the Red Guards and head of their short-lived government, with dictatorial powers, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Guards</a> under General Mannerheim and the Senate held northern Finland.</p><p>In Soviet Russia he succeeded Yrjö Sirola as chairman of the Finnish Communist Party and became an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. His decline began in 1929, when he and his wife Hanna Malm lost a briefcase of secret papers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>; the Swedish police seized it on a tip from the Finnish state police, and sixty-five communist agents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> were arrested and prosecuted. The Comintern then rebuked the party for its passivity towards the far-right <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapua-movement">Lapua Movement</a>, and Malm was removed from the central committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manner and Malm blamed the failure of 1918 on the slow Social Democratic leaders, Manner among them, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, who under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> had withheld promised aid. When Malm made this public in 1932 and named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-wille-kuusinen">Otto Wille Kuusinen</a>, the party reacted severely; Manner recanted, and she was exiled for a year to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uhtua">Uhtua</a> in Soviet Karelia. He lost most of his posts in May 1934. Arrested in 1935, both were sent to labour camps. Malm drowned herself at Solovki on 8 August 1937, and Manner died at the Ukhtpechlag camp on 15 January 1939, officially of tuberculosis, though the historian Alexander Popov suggests radiation sickness from work with radium-bearing water. He was rehabilitated in 1957.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kullervo Manner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 28 January the Red Guards seized Helsinki, and the Finnish People's Delegation, chaired by <b>Kullervo Manner</b>, took power in the industrial south.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-socialist-workers-republic">Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kokemäki, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. Died in UkhtPechLag, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=61.26,22.35&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Kokemäki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5012 122 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kullervo Manner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5462 392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5462 392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=61.26,22.35&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
